======================================================================================================================
  vacuum energy inside R500 / mean density inside R500 = Omega_L/500 = 1.370e-03; cold fraction needed (6.8x baryons) = 0.872
  -> converting ALL the vacuum energy in the volume gives 1.57e-03 of the cold mass: 636x short
  an inflow at c through the R500 sphere (R = 1.0 Mpc, over 5 Gyr) must carry a fraction eps = 0.138 of the vacuum energy flux rho_L c
  an inflow at c through the R500 sphere (R = 1.0 Mpc, over 10 Gyr) must carry a fraction eps = 0.069 of the vacuum energy flux rho_L c
  an inflow at c through the R500 sphere (R = 1.4 Mpc, over 5 Gyr) must carry a fraction eps = 0.194 of the vacuum energy flux rho_L c
  an inflow at c through the R500 sphere (R = 1.4 Mpc, over 10 Gyr) must carry a fraction eps = 0.097 of the vacuum energy flux rho_L c
  compare CFG176: a w = -1 vacuum carries NO flux (eps = 0 exactly); a NEC-respecting non-Lambda medium carries at most 0.078 of the cosmic
  column (0.090 NEC-only, CFG195), a cosmic-mean, perfect-fluid, integrated number, not a per-cluster convergent inflow.
  CFG131 D1: for a Lorentz-invariant vacuum, d_mu rho_L = Q u_mu, so rho_L is uniform on the cold fluid's slices and Q cannot be localised;
  CFG131 A5: in a stationary halo the energy equation forces Q = 0.
